module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.generic_coproduct_argument_unjudged_at_declared_return

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data generic_coproduct_argument_unjudged_at_declared_return: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "generic_coproduct_argument_unjudged_at_declared_return" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a fn declared to return one instantiation of a generic coproduct (`Outcome<Node>`) was admitted while its body produced another (`Outcome<ResolvedTree record>`).** DESIGN section 4b puts `values inhabit declared types` on the ordinary compiler floor, so this is below the floor, not a rung. Found by `wise-bat-862` in gunbc#12432, where about 35 test helpers carried the mismatch and nothing refused.",

    "THE CHAIN, per DESIGN section 6b. `v1.compiler.infer` `declared_type_conformance_diags` is the one judgment the declared-return and data-initializer positions consult. Its arms are: ground kernels and one-peel element collections; `nominal_product_inhabitance_refusal`, which compares applied type arguments through `applied_type_arguments_conflict` but abstains on any head that is not a declared PRODUCT; `coproduct_payload_where_parent_required`, which excludes generic coproducts by its own contract; the callable arms; and a terminal `!both_ground => []`. An application of a generic coproduct matches no judging arm, and it is never ground, so it reaches the terminal arm and is admitted by fallthrough. The arguments were never dropped by a comparison; no arm existed to compare them. The product arm's own note had filed this as the separate generic half of the inhabitance defect.",

    "REPAIR: `nominal_coproduct_applied_argument_conflict`, a sibling arm that consumes the two authorities the product arm already uses: `nominal_product_head_names_agree` for constructor identity and `applied_type_arguments_conflict` for the fail-open per-argument relation, where a type variable, an optional, a callable or a nested application is never read as a disagreement. It refuses only two applications of ONE declared coproduct constructor whose resolved arguments are both established and differ. RED and positive control: `test.claim.declared_type_generic_coproduct_return_witness_test`.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor (silently admitted). RUNG AFTER REPAIR: structurally guaranteed for a single-level argument of a declared generic coproduct at the declared-return and data-initializer positions of the v1 seed. CEILING: structurally guaranteed (a wrong return is authorable in source). NOT COVERED, stated rather than implied: nested application arguments (`Outcome<List<A>>` against `Outcome<List<B>>`), optional and callable arguments, and early `return` exits only as far as they already route through the same judgment. v2 has the same class in a wider form: `v2.std.inhabitance` `PositionDeclaredReturn` is declared, but no `DeclaredTypeObligation` producer in `v2.compiler.infer` emits it, so v2 judges no declared return at all. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS A CAPABILITY: one inhabitance judgment that recurses over applied type arguments of ANY established head (product, coproduct, nested application), sufficient to delete both head-kind-keyed argument arms, AND a v2 declared-return obligation producer consulting it.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
